import { CodeBlock } from '@/components/code-block'
import { TipBox } from '@/components/course/tip-box'
import { Exercise } from '@/components/course/exercise'

export default function Lesson() {
  return (
    <>
      <p>Go ships a test runner, an assertion-free testing philosophy, a benchmark
harness, a coverage tool, a fuzzer and a race detector. There is nothing to
install and nothing to configure. After a JavaScript project&apos;s testing setup,
this takes some adjusting to, mostly because you keep looking for the config
file.</p>

      <h2>The rules</h2>

      <p>A test file ends in <code>_test.go</code>, sits beside the code it tests, and contains
functions named <code>TestXxx</code> taking <code>*testing.T</code>.</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`// math.go
package math

func Add(a, b int) int { return a + b }`} />

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`// math_test.go
package math

import "testing"

func TestAdd(t *testing.T) {
    got := Add(2, 3)
    if got != 5 {
        t.Errorf("Add(2, 3) = %d, want 5", got)
    }
}`} />

      <CodeBlock language="bash" code={`go test ./...`} />

      <CodeBlock language="text" code={`ok      github.com/you/myapp/math    0.002s`} />

      <TipBox tone="info">
        <p><strong>If you know JavaScript</strong></p>
        <p>No <code>describe</code>, no <code>it</code>, no <code>expect</code>. A test is a function, and it fails by
calling <code>t.Errorf</code>. There is no assertion library in the standard library,
and this is deliberate: the Go team&apos;s position is that <code>if got != want</code> is
clearer than learning which of <code>toBe</code>, <code>toEqual</code> and <code>toStrictEqual</code> you
wanted.</p>
        <p>You will still meet <code>testify</code> in real codebases, which adds
<code>assert.Equal(t, want, got)</code>. It is popular and it is fine. Start without it.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <h2>Error messages are the whole skill</h2>

      <p>A failing test must tell you what happened without opening the source. The
convention is <code>got, want</code>:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`t.Errorf("Add(2, 3) = %d, want 5", got)`} />

      <CodeBlock language="text" code={`math_test.go:8: Add(2, 3) = 6, want 5`} />

      <p>That line names the input, the result and the expectation. Compare it to
<code>t.Error("failed")</code>, which tells you nothing you did not already know.</p>

      <h2>Errorf against Fatalf</h2>

      <p><code>t.Errorf</code> records a failure and <strong>keeps going</strong>. <code>t.Fatalf</code> records it and stops
the test function immediately.</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`u, err := Load(1)
if err != nil {
    t.Fatalf("Load(1): unexpected error: %v", err)   // stop: u is unusable
}
if u.Name != "Ada" {
    t.Errorf("Name = %q, want Ada", u.Name)          // keep checking
}`} />

      <p>Use <code>Fatalf</code> when continuing would panic or produce noise. Use <code>Errorf</code> when
you want to see every failure in one run.</p>

      <h2>Table-driven tests</h2>

      <p>This is the dominant Go testing idiom. One test function, a slice of cases.</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`func TestSlugify(t *testing.T) {
    tests := []struct {
        name string
        in   string
        want string
    }{
        {"simple", "Hello World", "hello-world"},
        {"punctuation", "Hello, World!", "hello-world"},
        {"already a slug", "hello-world", "hello-world"},
        {"leading space", "  padded  ", "padded"},
        {"empty", "", ""},
        {"unicode", "Café Noir", "cafe-noir"},
    }

    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            if got := Slugify(tt.in); got != tt.want {
                t.Errorf("Slugify(%q) = %q, want %q", tt.in, got, tt.want)
            }
        })
    }
}`} />

      <p><code>t.Run</code> creates a subtest with its own name, so output identifies the case:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="text" code={`--- FAIL: TestSlugify (0.00s)
    --- FAIL: TestSlugify/unicode (0.00s)
        slug_test.go:21: Slugify("Café Noir") =
            "caf-noir", want "cafe-noir"`} />

      <p>You can run one case directly:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="bash" code={`go test -run 'TestSlugify/unicode' ./...`} />

      <TipBox tone="info">
        <p><strong>If you know JavaScript</strong></p>
        <p>This replaces <code>describe.each</code> and <code>test.each</code>. The advantage over both is
that the cases are an ordinary slice of an ordinary struct, so adding a field
to the table is a normal code change and the compiler checks every row.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <h2>Comparing things that are not numbers</h2>

      <p><code>==</code> works on structs of comparable fields. For slices, maps and nested
structs, use <code>reflect.DeepEqual</code> or, much better, <code>go-cmp</code>:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`import "github.com/google/go-cmp/cmp"

if diff := cmp.Diff(want, got); diff != "" {
    t.Errorf("Load() mismatch (-want +got):\\n%s", diff)
}`} />

      <CodeBlock language="text" code={`user_test.go:31: Load() mismatch (-want +got):
      user.User{
            ID:    1,
    -       Name:  "Ada",
    +       Name:  "ada",
            Email: "ada@example.com",
      }`} />

      <p>A readable diff instead of <code>false</code>. This is the one testing dependency worth
adding on day one.</p>

      <h2>Helpers and t.Helper</h2>

      <p>A helper that reports failures should call <code>t.Helper()</code> so the line number
points at the <strong>caller</strong>:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`func mustLoad(t *testing.T, id int) *User {
    t.Helper()                       // failures blame the caller's line
    u, err := Load(id)
    if err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("Load(%d): %v", id, err)
    }
    return u
}`} />

      <p>Without it, every failure points at the line inside <code>mustLoad</code>, which is
useless when ten tests call it.</p>

      <h2>Setup and cleanup</h2>

      <p><code>t.Cleanup</code> registers work to run when the test finishes, including on failure.</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`func newTestDB(t *testing.T) *sql.DB {
    t.Helper()
    db, err := sql.Open("sqlite", ":memory:")
    if err != nil {
        t.Fatal(err)
    }
    t.Cleanup(func() { db.Close() })    // runs even if the test fails
    return db
}`} />

      <p>For package-wide setup, <code>TestMain</code>:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`func TestMain(m *testing.M) {
    // before all tests
    code := m.Run()
    // after all tests
    os.Exit(code)
}`} />

      <TipBox tone="info">
        <p><strong>If you know JavaScript</strong></p>
        <p><code>t.Cleanup</code> is <code>afterEach</code>, scoped to one test and registered where the
resource is created rather than in a separate block. <code>TestMain</code> is
<code>globalSetup</code> and <code>globalTeardown</code>. There is no <code>beforeEach</code>: you call a
helper at the top of each test, which is more typing and means a reader can
see what a test depends on without scrolling.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <h2>Parallel tests</h2>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`func TestSomething(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()
    // ...
}`} />

      <p>Tests in different packages already run in parallel. <code>t.Parallel()</code> marks tests
<strong>within</strong> a package as safe to interleave.</p>

      <TipBox tone="warning">
        <p><strong>Watch out</strong></p>
        <p>A parallel test must not share mutable state with another test. Writing to
the same database table, the same temp file or the same package-level
variable will fail intermittently, which is worse than failing reliably.</p>
        <p>Combine <code>t.Parallel()</code> with <code>-race</code> so that a shared-state mistake is
reported rather than merely flaky.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <h2>Testing errors</h2>

      <p>Test the <strong>kind</strong>, not the message. Message text is not a contract.</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`// Brittle: breaks when someone improves the wording.
if err.Error() != "user not found" { ... }

// Correct.
if !errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound) {
    t.Errorf("got %v, want ErrNotFound", err)
}`} />

      <p>And always test that an error <strong>is</strong> returned when you expect one:</p>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`{"missing email", "", "", true},   // a wantErr field in the table
...
_, err := Create(tt.name, tt.email)
if (err != nil) != tt.wantErr {
    t.Errorf("Create() error = %v, wantErr %v", err, tt.wantErr)
}`} />

      <h2>Coverage</h2>

      <CodeBlock language="bash" code={`go test -cover ./...
go test -coverprofile=cover.out ./... && go tool cover -html=cover.out`} />

      <p>The second opens a browser showing every line in green or red. It is the
fastest way to find the branch nobody tested.</p>

      <TipBox tone="warning">
        <p><strong>Watch out</strong></p>
        <p>Coverage measures lines executed, not behaviour verified. A test that calls
every function and asserts nothing reports high coverage and proves nothing.
Use it to find what you <strong>missed</strong>, never as a target to hit.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <h2>Benchmarks</h2>

      <CodeBlock language="go" code={`func BenchmarkSlugify(b *testing.B) {
    for b.Loop() {
        Slugify("Hello, World! This is a title")
    }
}`} />

      <CodeBlock language="bash" code={`go test -bench=. -benchmem ./...`} />

      <CodeBlock language="text" code={`BenchmarkSlugify-8    2419371    489.2 ns/op    144 B/op    3 allocs/op`} />

      <p>Operations per second, nanoseconds each, bytes allocated and allocation count.
<code>b.Loop()</code> replaced the older <code>for i := 0; i &lt; b.N; i++</code> in Go 1.24 and is
harder to get wrong, because it stops the compiler optimising your work away.</p>

      <TipBox tone="success">
        <p><strong>Project 17: test the user store properly</strong></p>
        <p>Take the store from chapter eleven and give it a real test suite: table
driven, testing error kinds rather than messages, with a helper and cleanup.</p>
        <CodeBlock language="go" code={`package store

import (
    "errors"
    "testing"
)

func newStore(t *testing.T) *Store {
    t.Helper()
    s := NewStore()
    t.Cleanup(func() {
        // Nothing to close here, but the hook is where a real test would
        // drop a temp database. Wire it now so it is not retrofitted
        // later.
    })
    return s
}

func TestCreate(t *testing.T) {
    tests := []struct {
        name    string
        user    string
        email   string
        wantErr error       // nil, a sentinel, or errSomeValidation
        field   string      // for validation errors
    }{
        {"valid", "Ada", "ada@example.com", nil, ""},
        {"empty name", "", "x@example.com", errValidation, "name"},
        {"bad email", "Ada", "not-an-email", errValidation, "email"},
    }

    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            t.Parallel()
            s := newStore(t)

            u, err := s.Create(tt.user, tt.email)

            if tt.wantErr == nil {
                if err != nil {
                    t.Fatalf("Create(%q, %q): unexpected error: %v",
                        tt.user, tt.email, err)
                }
                if u.Name != tt.user {
                    t.Errorf("Name = %q, want %q", u.Name, tt.user)
                }
                if u.ID == 0 {
                    t.Error("ID was not assigned")
                }
                return
            }

            if err == nil {
                t.Fatalf("Create(%q, %q): want error, got nil",
                    tt.user, tt.email)
            }
            var ve *ValidationError
            if !errors.As(err, &ve) {
                t.Fatalf("got %v, want a *ValidationError", err)
            }
            if ve.Field != tt.field {
                t.Errorf("Field = %q, want %q", ve.Field, tt.field)
            }
        })
    }
}

func TestCreateDuplicateEmail(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()
    s := newStore(t)

    if _, err := s.Create("Ada", "ada@example.com"); err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("first create: %v", err)
    }
    _, err := s.Create("Grace", "ada@example.com")

    // The identity of the failure is the contract. The wording is not.
    if !errors.Is(err, ErrConflict) {
        t.Errorf("got %v, want ErrConflict", err)
    }
}

func TestGetMissing(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()
    s := newStore(t)

    if _, err := s.Get(999); !errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound) {
        t.Errorf("got %v, want ErrNotFound", err)
    }
}`} />
        <p>Run it four ways and notice what each tells you.</p>
        <p><code>go test ./...</code> gives you pass or fail. <code>go test -v ./...</code> names every
subtest, which is how you confirm the table ran every row rather than
silently skipping. <code>go test -race ./...</code> would catch the shared-state bug if
<code>newStore</code> returned a package-level store instead of a fresh one, which is
the mistake <code>t.Parallel()</code> makes dangerous. And <code>go test -cover ./...</code> tells
you which branch of <code>Create</code> you forgot.</p>
        <p>The important design point is in <code>TestCreateDuplicateEmail</code>: it asserts
<code>errors.Is(err, ErrConflict)</code> and never looks at the message. Change the
wording of that error and the test still passes, which is correct, because
the wording was never the promise.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <TipBox tone="info">
        <p><strong>Your turn</strong></p>
        <p>Add a benchmark for <code>Create</code> and run it with <code>-benchmem</code>. Then change the
duplicate-email check from a linear scan to a second map keyed by email, and
run the benchmark again with a thousand existing users. The allocation count
and the nanoseconds per operation will both move, and seeing a design change
show up as a number is the habit this chapter is really teaching.</p>
      </TipBox>

      <h2>Assignment</h2>
      <p>Commit your work with the message <code>Testing - Assignment One</code>, exactly as written, so the leaderboard can score it.</p>
      <Exercise
        prompt={<>
            <p>Go back and test something you already wrote.</p>
            <p>Take your chapter eleven <code>store</code> package and bring it to a state you would
defend in review.</p>
            <p><strong>One.</strong> Table-driven tests for every exported function, including the error
cases, with the table naming each case.</p>
            <p><strong>Two.</strong> A failure message that says what it wanted and what it got. Break one
test on purpose and check the output names the case.</p>
            <p><strong>Three.</strong> A <code>t.Helper()</code> assertion helper, and <code>t.Cleanup</code> for anything the
test created.</p>
            <p><strong>Four.</strong> Coverage, recorded rather than chased:</p>
            <CodeBlock language="bash" code={`go test -cover ./... | tee COVERAGE.txt`} />
            <p><strong>Five.</strong> One benchmark, with <code>-benchmem</code>, and one sentence on whether the
allocations surprised you.</p>
        </>}
        solution={<p>Work it through yourself first. The chapter above has everything you need, and the leaderboard scores the commit, not the answer.</p>}
      />
    </>
  )
}
